    "https://usccareers.usc.edu/job/los-angeles/research-lab-specialist/1209/101067474880": {
      "title": "Research Lab Specialist",
      "description": "Research Lab Specialist at USC Skip to main content Life at USC Career Areas Faculty Keck Medicine of USC Auxiliary Services Information Technology Services Human Resources, Ethics, and Compliance USC Advancement Current Employees Returning Applicants Saved Jobs 0 Job Alerts Join Our Trojan Community At USC, we are united by shared values and a bold vision for the future. Join a workplace where your role has meaning, your growth is supported, and your impact is felt across campus, Los Angeles, and the world. Search by Keyword Search Jobs Search Jobs Search by Keyword Location Radius Select Radius 5 miles 15 miles 25 miles 35 miles 50 miles Search Jobs Research Lab Specialist Los Angeles, CA Apply Save Job Current employees apply here Job ID REQ20180867 Category Laboratory Helpful Information for Applicants External Applicants Internal Applicants (Current USC Employees) USC Department of Stem Cell Biology and Regenerative Medicine is seeking a Research Lab Specialist. Working directly under the supervision of the Megan McCain, the Research Lab Specialist works closely with graduate students and postdoctoral researchers to support projects related to developing and using microphysiological systems for cardiac disease modeling. The successful candidate will be motivated, pro-active, resourceful, and detail-oriented.  The position requires excellent organizational and communication skills with the ability to be decisive and adaptable.  Experience in aseptic technique is required. Experience in human stem cell or primary cell culture, flow cytometry, microscopy, and/or fabrication of microfluidic devices is preferred. Essential job duties include, but are not limited to: Maintenance, expansion, differentiation, and cryopreservation of human stem cells and primary cells Inventory and ordering lab supplies Maintenance of equipment, including cell culture incubators and biosafety cabinets Protocol documentation Handling and disposing of hazardous waste Assistance with data management Training new lab personnel Required Qualifications: Ability to multi-task effectively and demonstrate strong organizational skills A cooperative and team-oriented attitude Experience in aseptic technique 5 years of experience working in lab Preferred Qualifications: Experience in human stem cell or primary cell culture, flow cytometry, microscopy, and/or fabrication of microfluidic devices 7 years of lab experience Follows established USC and department policies, procedures, objectives, performance improvement, attendance, safety, environmental, and infection control guidelines, including adherence to the workplace Code of Conduct and Compliance Plan. This position is on-site and employee must report to work at the USC University Park Campus in Los Angeles, CA when scheduled. Work hours and on-site days may be subject to change depending on business needs. The annual budgeted salary range for this position is $ 72,504.01 - $ 99,997.09 When extending an offer of employment, the University of Southern California considers factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate's work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, federal, state and local laws, contractual stipulations, grant funding, as well as external market and organizational considerations. Minimum Education: Related graduate study, Combined experience/education as substitute for minimum education\nMinimum Experience: 5 years\nMinimum Field of Expertise: Directly related education and experience in research specialization with advanced knowledge of equipment, procedures and analysis methods and ability to supervise on a regular or project basis Job ID REQ20180867 Posted Date 09/24/2026 Apply Save Job Current employees apply here SHARE Facebook X LinkedIn Email Related Content Learn More August 04, 2025 USC Today Explore the latest stories of impact, innovation, and Trojan spirit shaping USC and the world