    "https://usccareers.usc.edu/job/los-angeles/vice-dean-finance-and-administration-and-chief-financial-officer/1209/101096857504": {
      "title": "Vice Dean, Finance & Administration and Chief Financial Officer",
      "description": "Vice Dean, Finance & Administration and Chief Financial Officer at USC We use cookies and other tracking technologies to support navigation, to improve our products and services, to support our marketing activities and to provide content from third parties.To manage your preferences, select \"Manage Settings\" or choose \"Accept\" to consent to the use of Cookies. Accept Manage Settings Skip to main content Life at USC Career Areas Faculty Keck Medicine of USC Auxiliary Services Information Technology Services Human Resources, Ethics, and Compliance USC Advancement Current Employees Returning Applicants Saved Jobs 0 Job Alerts Join Our Trojan Community At USC, we are united by shared values and a bold vision for the future. Join a workplace where your role has meaning, your growth is supported, and your impact is felt across campus, Los Angeles, and the world. Search by Keyword Matching job links will appear below as you type. Search Jobs Search Jobs Search by Keyword Location When autocomplete results are available use up and down arrows to review and enter to select. Touch device users, explore by touch or with swipe gestures. Radius Select Radius 5 miles 15 miles 25 miles 35 miles 50 miles Search Jobs Vice Dean, Finance & Administration and Chief Financial Officer Los Angeles, CA Apply Save Job Current employees apply here Job ID REQ20181174 Category Business and Financial Management Helpful Information for Applicants External Applicants Internal Applicants (Current USC Employees) USC Marshall School of Business Vice Dean of Finance and Administration and Chief Financial Officer The USC Marshall School of Business seeks an accomplished, collaborative, and forward-looking leader to serve as Vice Dean of Finance and Administration and Chief Financial Officer . Reporting to the Interim Dean and serving as a key member of the school’s senior leadership team, the Vice Dean will provide strategic leadership across Marshall’s financial and administrative operations and help position the school for continued academic excellence, innovation, and long-term success. This is a broad and influential leadership role extending well beyond traditional financial management. The Vice Dean will serve as a trusted strategic partner to the Dean, Vice Deans, Associate Deans, faculty leaders, and administrative leadership, helping the school evaluate opportunities, navigate complex tradeoffs, and align its resources and operations with its academic mission and strategic priorities. The Vice Dean will bring together financial insight, operational expertise, and a deep understanding of the higher education environment. They will ensure that school leaders have timely, relevant, and actionable information to support sound decisions. The successful candidate will anticipate emerging needs, translate complex financial and operational matters into clear options, and balance analytical rigor with the responsiveness required in a dynamic academic institution. The Vice Dean oversees Administrative Services, Financial Services, Human Resources and Payroll, Information Technology and Facilities. They will lead and develop a multidisciplinary organization, establish clear expectations and accountability, strengthen leadership capacity, and build sustainable systems that support continuity and effective service across the school. Equally important, the Vice Dean will be a visible and trusted member of the Marshall community. They will understand and value the distinct perspectives of faculty and staff, approach challenges with curiosity and flexibility, and cultivate relationships grounded in competence, transparency, respect, and a shared commitment to the school’s mission. The role also represents Marshall in university-wide planning and works closely with central partners across finance, human resources, information technology, facilities, research administration, and other administrative functions. Key Responsibilities Serve as a trusted strategic advisor to the Dean and senior leadership team on financial strategy, resource allocation, operational priorities, and long-range planning. Align financial and operational decisions with Marshall’s academic mission, strategic objectives, and institutional priorities. Provide timely financial analysis, forecasts, scenario planning, and recommendations that help leaders evaluate opportunities, risks, and tradeoffs. Lead the development and oversight of the school’s annual budget, multi-year financial plans, and business plans for new programs and strategic initiatives. Provide strategic oversight of finance and budget, human resources and payroll, information technology, facilities, administrative services, and sponsored projects administration. Lead and develop a multidisciplinary team by establishing clear expectations, strengthening accountability, developing talent, and supporting succession planning. Guide organizational planning, including structures, staffing strategies, operational processes, and service-delivery models. Partner effectively with faculty, staff, school leaders, and central university offices to address complex needs and advance shared priorities. Build trust across the Marshall community through collaborative leadership, transparent communication, sound judgment, and consistent follow-through. Perform other related duties as assigned. The university reserves the right to add or change duties at any time. Minimum Qualifications Bachelor’s degree in a related field. Ten years of progressively responsible experience in finance, administration, or a related area, including significant leadership experience in higher education. Demonstrated expertise in budgeting, financial planning and analysis, financial controls, resource allocation, and long-range planning. Experience leading people and multiple administrative functions within a large, complex organization. Understanding of the academic enterprise, including faculty needs, academic programs, research, fundraising, scholarships, and university resource-allocation models. Demonstrated success advising senior leaders, evaluating competing priorities, and aligning financial and operational decisions with institutional goals. Strong analytical, strategic-planning, relationship-building, and communication skills, with a record of developing teams and fostering accountability. Thorough knowledge of management principles and applicable financial, employment, and regulatory requirements. Preferred Qualifications Master of Business Administration or another relevant advanced degree. Fifteen years of relevant experience, including senior leadership within a complex academic institution. Experience implementing technology, data analytics, and management reporting to improve decision-making and organizational performance. Demonstrated success leading organizational change while navigating complex institutional, political, and cultural considerations. Proven ability to recruit, develop, and retain high-performing leadership teams and sustain new ways of working. Leadership Attributes The successful candidate will be: A strategic and pragmatic advisor who anticipates needs, clarifies options, and helps leaders make informed decisions. A well-rounded institutional leader who understands how finance and operations support the academic mission. A collaborative, flexible partner who values faculty and staff perspectives and builds trust across a complex organization. A strong people leader who sets clear expectations, develops talent, and builds accountable, resilient teams. A transparent and thoughtful communicator who leads with sound judgment, integrity, kindness, and respect. Why Join the USC Marshall School of Business? USC Marshall is a globally recognized business school distinguished by its commitment to academic excellence, real-world learning, technological fluency, human-centered leadership, and interdisciplinary collaboration. Located in Los Angeles, one of the world’s most dynamic and diverse centers of business, innovation, and culture, Marshall draws strength from its connections to leading industries, its global perspective, and the extraordinary reach of the Trojan Network. The Vice Dean will join an ambitious academic community preparing students to lead in an increasingly complex and rapidly evolving world. This is a consequential opportunity to partner with Marshall’s leadership, faculty, and staff at an important moment in the school’s evolution; strengthen the systems and teams that enable its mission; and help shape its future through thoughtful stewardship, strategic insight, and trusted leadership. For more information about the USC Marshall School of Business, visit www.marshall.usc.edu (opens in new window) . Required Documents and Additional Information Resume and cover letter required and may be submitted as one attachment. The annual base salary range for this position is $390,000–$437,000. When extending an offer of employment, the University of Southern California considers factors such as, but not limited to, the scope and responsibilities of the position; the candidate’s work experience, education, training, and key skills; internal peer alignment; federal, state, and local laws; contractual stipulations; grant funding; and external market and organizational considerations. To support faculty and staff well-being, USC provides benefits-eligible employees with a broad range of benefits and resources designed to help protect their and their dependents’ health, financial well-being, and future. These benefits are part of USC’s overall compensation and total rewards package. The University of Southern California is an Equal Opportunity Employer. #LI-Onsite Minimum Education: Bachelor's Degree\nMinimum Experience: 10 years\nMinimum Field of Expertise: Progressively increasing leadership responsibilities in budget, financial control, cash flow analysis and debt planning. Progressively increasing leadership, planning and management experience in human resources field. Demonstrated strong organization, planning, analytical, innovative, critical thinking, strategic, collaboration, interpersonal and relationship building skills are essential. Thorough knowledge of management principles, budgeting, finance and applicable laws/regulations.\n\nPreferred Education: Masters of Business Administration\nPreferred Experience: 15 years\nPreferred Field of Expertise: Knowledge of higher education operations and environment in addition to budget, financial control, cash flow analysis and debt planning, and human resources Job ID REQ20181174 Posted Date 09/24/2026 Apply Save Job Current employees apply here SHARE Facebook X LinkedIn Email Related Content Mission Statement USC’s central mission is the development of human beings and society as a whole through the cultivation and enrichment of the human mind and spirit. Learn More July 28, 2025 Mission & Values Blog - Hub Page Related Content Our Communities Rooted in LA, USC partners with local communities to uplift education, well-being, and opportunity across Southern California. Learn More July 28, 2025 Culture & Community Blog - Hub Page Related Content USC Today Explore the latest stories of impact, innovation, and Trojan spirit shaping USC and the world beyond. Learn More July 28, 2025 USC News Blog - Hub Page Related Content Load more Jobs for You Featured Jobs Saved Jobs Viewed Jobs Save Job Radiology Technologist - Arcadia Orthopaedic Surgery Clinic - Full Time 8 Hour Days (Non-Exempt) (Non-Union) Arcadia, California Save Job RN-Perioperative Clinical Educator - Full Time 10 Hours (exempt, Non-Union) Arcadia, California Save Job Registered Nurse - 6th Floor Med/Surg Telemetry - Full Time 12 Hour Night Shift (Union) Glendale, California Save Job Instructor of Clinical Physical Therapy (Keck Hospital) Los Angeles, California No Saved Jobs. View All Jobs No Recently Viewed Jobs. View All Jobs Sign Up for Job Alerts